Z||000007||Ken Wittlief||06-26-2001||09:26 AM||||Ogre, <BR>  cool! I hadnt thought of that - tracking miles over several tanks accumalatively averages out the 1 gallon errors.<P> I cant see any logical reason why mileage would be worst if you put the last one or two gallons in, but there are possibliities:<P> 1. when you click click click the handle maybe the pump doesnt record acutal volume correctly - it tells you it added 2 gallons when it only let one through.<P> 2. your tanks leaks when its full, so the top gallon or so gadually spills out on sharp turns or acceleration.<P> ...?! thats all I can think of. the extra 10 lbs of weight for the last 2 gallons of fuel cant be it. I dont think the fuel pump or pressure return valve would act any differently with a full tank. If the vapor canister is sucking fuel into the engine I think you would notice the soot...<P>  ?!||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||06-26-2001||08:46 PM||||Well, As I left out above.... but I think I put it in the other thread....<P>My tank was just tested a month ago by the state of DE DMV. They do a pressure test on the tank and gas cap for evap emissions control. The test passed easily. I'm pretty sure there are no holes anywhere in the system.<P>There is no evidence of a seeping joint.<P>One thing that is posible is the over stuffed tank messes with fuel pressure on the TBI. These only run 10-14PSI. Back pressure in the return line could be a problem.<P>Every thing else on the motor is perfect. Most of it's new.<P>The only change was stuffing the tank or not.<P>It's something to try... ||||reg||1
